# Erdős Problem #99 — low-`t` pentagon metric amplification

**Date:** 2026-09-14  
**Target:** C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch  
**Dependencies:** low-`t` pentagon sea; empty-pentagon trapezoid endpoint; flat-pentagon chain extraction; pentagonal two-contact tooth extraction  
**Claim class:** proof-only structural synthesis; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**External literature:** no new import

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The existing flat-chain and two-contact-tooth theorems use only the coarse all-Type-II
exceptional-face floor.  In the low-`t` regime the actual pentagon count is much larger.
On the small true force-hull residual branch, that stronger count passes directly to
metric source objects.

Put

\[
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad C_0:=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+0.00042,
\]

and on a convergent Type-II subsequence write

\[
t:=\lim{q-k\over D},\qquad
\delta:=\lim{b-P_F\over D},\qquad
\kappa:=\lim{k\over D}.
\]

Define

\[
P(t,\delta,\kappa)
:=\pi+C_0-{5t\over2}+\delta+{\kappa\over2}.
\tag{MA.1}
\]

The low-`t` pentagon theorem gives

\[
\boxed{{p_5\over D}\ge P(t,\delta,\kappa)-o(1).}
\tag{MA.2}
\]

Assume additionally

\[
R_F=o(D).
\tag{MA.3}
\]

Then there is a scale `epsilon_D->0` for which

\[
\boxed{
{V_{flat}\over D}\ge {P(t,\delta,\kappa)\over5}-o(1),
}
\tag{MA.4}
\]

\[
\boxed{
{C_{flat}\over D}\ge {P(t,\delta,\kappa)\over13}-o(1),
}
\tag{MA.5}
\]

where `V_flat` counts distinct positive-core source vertices having two exact unit
contacts whose angle tends to `pi`, and `C_flat` counts a pairwise vertex-disjoint
family of the corresponding length-two contact chains.

For every fixed `eta>0`, let `H_eta` be the maximum number of endpoint-disjoint hidden
chords from pentagonal two-contact teeth, and let `T_eta` be the maximum number of
vertex-disjoint such teeth.  Then

\[
\boxed{
{H_\eta\over D}\ge {P(t,\delta,\kappa)\over9}-o(1),
\qquad
{T_\eta\over D}\ge {P(t,\delta,\kappa)\over13}-o(1).
}
\tag{MA.6}
\]

Every hidden chord counted by `H_eta` has length in `(1,1+eta)` and has endpoints with
a common source neighbor joined to each by an exact unit contact.

No assumption `Q-f=o(D)` is needed in (MA.4)--(MA.6).  The low-`t` pentagon theorem
has already paid for the nonpentagonal exceptional faces.

## 1. Almost every low-`t` pentagon is metrically good when `R_F=o(D)`

Fix `epsilon>0`.  The empty-pentagon trapezoid theorem and Wegner first-jump argument
supply a threshold `theta(epsilon)>0` such that a pentagon with local residual below
`theta(epsilon)` is isolate-free and `epsilon`-close to the flat unit trapezoid.

All local exceptional-face residuals are nonnegative and their sum is at most `R_F`.
Thus (MA.3) implies that only

\[
{R_F\over\theta(\epsilon)}=o(D)
\]

pentagons fail the threshold.  If `G_epsilon` is the number of good pentagons, then
(MA.2) gives

\[
\boxed{
{G_\epsilon\over D}\ge P(t,\delta,\kappa)-o(1).
}
\tag{MA.7}
\]

This is the composition missing from the earlier coarse metric-extraction notes.

## 2. Near-straight exact-contact chains

Select the distinguished almost-flat middle vertex from each good pentagon.  A source
vertex belongs to at most five bounded face sectors in the triangle-free unit-contact
graph.  Hence

\[
G_\epsilon\le5V_{flat}(\epsilon).
\tag{MA.8}
\]

For each good pentagon select its three-vertex boundary path through that middle
vertex.  Two selected paths conflict when they share a source vertex.  Each path has
three vertices and each source vertex occurs in at most five selected face paths, so
the conflict graph has degree at most `3(5-1)=12`.  Therefore

\[
C_{flat}(\epsilon)\ge {G_\epsilon\over13}.
\tag{MA.9}
\]

Equations (MA.7)--(MA.9) prove the fixed-`epsilon` versions of (MA.4)--(MA.5).
Choosing `epsilon_D->0` sufficiently slowly preserves the same first-order constants.

## 3. Two-contact near-equilateral teeth

In the flat-trapezoid equality model, each leaf supplies a two-edge boundary path of
exact unit contacts whose hidden chord is also unit.  Trapezoid stability therefore
implies that for every fixed `eta>0`, every sufficiently low-residual good pentagon
contains a tooth whose hidden chord has length below `1+eta`.  The hidden chord is
strictly longer than one in an actual exceptional face, since a unit chord would be a
contact splitting the face and would create the forbidden unit triangle.

Choose one tooth per good pentagon.  The conflict graph on hidden chord endpoints has
maximum degree at most eight, so at least `G_eta/9` chosen hidden chords are
endpoint-disjoint.  The conflict graph on whole three-vertex teeth has maximum degree
at most twelve, so at least `G_eta/13` teeth are vertex-disjoint.  Equation (MA.7)
proves (MA.6).

## 4. Current-floor constants

Let

\[
C_{146}:={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right),
\qquad
t_{42}:=C_{146}+0.0013751
=1.8208886379794447\ldots .
\]

At the most permissive endpoint profile `delta=kappa=0`,

\[
P(t_{42},0,0)=0.4944166723690961\ldots .
\tag{MA.10}
\]

Consequently every `R_F=o(D)` survivor at this floor satisfies

\[
\boxed{{V_{flat}\over D}\ge0.0988833344738192\ldots-o(1),}
\tag{MA.11}
\]

\[
\boxed{
{C_{flat}\over D},\ {T_\eta\over D}
\ge0.0380320517206997\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{MA.12}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{{H_\eta\over D}\ge0.0549351858187884\ldots-o(1).}
\tag{MA.13}
\]

These coefficients improve the corresponding coarse all-Type-II small-residual floors
by factors

\[
{0.4944166723\ldots\over0.1671246434\ldots}>2.95.
\tag{MA.14}
\]

The concurrent low-`t` two-window theorem also gives, at the same profile,

\[
{T_{off}(u,v;\epsilon)\over D}
\ge0.247208336184549\ldots-o(1)
\]

for every fixed pair of narrow projective windows, while the low-`t` topology theorem
gives

\[
{L_{OO}\over D}\ge0.320334770872156\ldots-o(1)
\]

actual outer-to-outer trains, each two-sidedly braced by diameter edges.  Thus all of
these local metric and global train populations occur simultaneously in the hard
low-`t`, small-residual corner.

## 5. Uniform `t<=1.8585` constants

If `t<=1.8585`, then after dropping `delta,kappa>=0`,

\[
P(t,\delta,\kappa)
\ge0.4003882673177079\ldots .
\tag{MA.15}
\]

Hence

\[
\boxed{{V_{flat}\over D}\ge0.0800776534635415\ldots-o(1),}
\tag{MA.16}
\]

\[
\boxed{
{C_{flat}\over D},\ {T_\eta\over D}
\ge0.0307990974859775\ldots-o(1),
}
\tag{MA.17}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{{H_\eta\over D}\ge0.0444875852575231\ldots-o(1).}
\tag{MA.18}
\]

The same band already has `T_off/D>=0.200194133658854...` for every two-window pair
and `L_OO/D>=0.132277960769379...`.

## 6. Frontier effect and scope

This theorem does not close C-II.  It makes the remaining low-`t`, small-residual
geometry much less permissive: a linear family of almost-straight exact-contact
chains, a linear family of structured almost-equilateral two-contact teeth, a
macroscopic train population outside every two projective windows, and a macroscopic
two-sidedly diameter-braced outer-train family must coexist.

A closing theorem can now target a simultaneous perturbation of the disjoint local
chains/teeth or a charge from their vanishing metric defects into the near-extremal
Woodall diameter graph.  This note does not assert such a deformation or charge.

Scope checks:

- (MA.2) uses the proof-only AG42 low-`t` pentagon theorem.
- (MA.4)--(MA.6) additionally require `R_F=o(D)`.
- No `Q-f=o(D)` assumption is used.
- All counted objects are finite-source vertices, edges, faces, and trains.
- The fixed-threshold statements are proved before diagonalizing the metric tolerance.
- No exact hidden contact, exact triangle, or same-cardinality strict sub-`D`
  competitor is claimed.

C-II and Level I remain open.
